# How to use this lesson

Three quantitative claims now sit on the page: a=α/(2π) at one loop (Lesson 75), a Lamb interval of order 10³ MHz (Lessons 76–77), and a few-percent running of α up to the Z (Lesson 68). This lesson places those claims next to the measurements, states what Lesson 15's “one part in a billion” actually referred to, and says what a genuine discrepancy would mean. Expect four to six hours.

## Learning objectives

- Quote a_e(one loop)=α/(2π)≈0.00116141 versus a_e(exp)=0.00115965218073(28), and name higher loops as the difference.

- Quote the Lamb interval 1057.845(9) MHz as a measured QED effect absent from the Dirac spectrum.

- Quote α⁻¹(0)≈137.036 versus α⁻¹(M_Z)≈127.95 once all charged fermions are included, of which this course computed only the electron logarithm's order of magnitude.

- State that a discrepancy remaining after higher orders and hadronic vacuum polarization would be extra amplitudes, not a failure of Lesson 67's mass renormalization.

